{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":1,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1474910976,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDU6SXNzdWUxNzkyODkxNDU=","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/921","origin":"github"},"title":"RFC:  mark non-redistributable materials","message":"Soon we will approach git annex meta-data use and I wondered if we should agree on some meta-data tags/fields to use, especially to signal which content should not be published by 'publish'\n- **proprietary** content, e.g. stimuli movie files\n- human subject **sensitive** (or **private**, **non-annonymized**) data, e.g. not defaced anatomicals (could make it explicit **non-defaced**)\n- data files which are generally non-redistributable since original source requires **authentication**\n\nSo I wondered if we should may be use those bold terms as annex metadata tags, and then by default not publish anything which has those tags... ?  \n\nactually -- since we might grow the number of such 'restrictions', what about making a metadata field \"redistribution-restrictions\" which would collect all such restrictions, so we could easily say to not publish anything which has any `redistribution-restrictions`, e.g. (here it is to match, but we could say `--not`; also here named `restrictions` but we could make it more descriptive with `redistribution-restrictions`)\n\n``` shell\n$\u003e git annex find --not --in here --metadata restrictions=\\*         \nstimulus/task002/orig/INDIANA_JONES_RAIDERS_LOST_ARK_part_1.m4v\n```\n\nwhat do you think @datalad/developers ?","files":null}]}